Roadmap of this fork
====================

Work planned on the 'storage' branch, on top of what CHANGES-FORK lists.
The order inside each section is the order of priority.

Everything here is built on SnapRAID, mergerfs and standard Linux tools,
under GPL-3.0-or-later.


Open items
==========

- Look at the reworked Replace dialog in a browser. Only its rules are tested.
- Guided step for an array left degraded by a recovery whose closing sync
  stopped on a full parity. Today the way out is to free space and then use
  Recover, "accept the disks as they are".
- When a disk is not mounted, name the disk and offer to mount or replace it.
  Never lead with the --force-empty hint.
- Extend the tests of disks of different sizes to xfs and btrfs data disks.
- Test with real multi-TB disks, and the 16 TiB limit of a parity file on ext4.
- Balance: when nothing can be moved because the files are recent, say so
  in the result instead of "Nothing more can be moved".


Features
========

1. Parity swap
   - Done: the parity, or one file of a split parity, copied to a larger
     disk, checked and switched to, with the old disk reused for files;
     offered from the parity row, the Disks page, the advisor and the Add
     dialog.

2. Fast disk and mover, what is left
   - Done: an SSD first in the pool, a mover by schedule, by fill level or
     on request, followed by the maintenance; folders that stay on it.
   - Two fast disks mirrored in front of the same pool. It needs the health
     of the mirror watched and reported, or it is a false comfort.

3. Protection window, what is left
   - Done: the files changed since the last sync, its age and the next one;
     the sync after the mover; the added/deleted ratio ('sync_ratio'); the
     sync when the changes pass a threshold ('sync_changes').

4. Shares, what is left
   - Done: SMB and NFS shares, users, recycle bin, permission reset, size of
     a share disk by disk, a folder or a share kept on chosen disks.
   - New files of a kept folder written straight to its disks (mergerfs
     "ep" policies or a pool of its own), in place of being brought back at
     night. Friendly names of the placement policies on the Pool tab.

5. Disk burn-in, what is left
   - Done: SMART short and long self-tests, a reading test of the whole
     disk, and a write and read back test for a new, empty disk.
   - Tried on loop devices only: try it on a real disk of some TB.

6. Empty a disk
   - Done: a disk emptied to the others of the pool, then taken out of the
     array in the same window; a folder moved to chosen disks.

7. Notification channels
   - Done: ntfy, Discord, Telegram, email and any web address, more of them
     at once, with a test, and pop-ups of the browser.
   - Web Push without the page open. It needs the pages served over HTTPS,
     and a key pair of the server: today the pages are plain HTTP.

8. History of sync and scrub
   - Done: duration and speed of every run, the errors found, repaired and
     lost of every run kept on disk, read and write history of the disks.

9. Encrypted disks
   - LUKS at format time, unlock at boot with a key file, state in the
     inventory.
   - Not built yet: the lab cannot open a LUKS volume (no device-mapper in
     its kernel), and a change that can lock people out of their files must
     be tried end to end first.

10. External disks and remote mounts
    - Done: a USB disk mounted for a backup and released; Open and Eject of
      any plugged disk; SMB and NFS folders as sources of backups.
    - Tried in the lab with a stand-in of mount.cifs: try real SMB and NFS
      servers (Windows, macOS, a NAS, Linux).

11. UPS
    - Done: sync and scrub stopped on battery, shutdown before the battery
      is empty (NUT, apcupsd).
    - Tried in the lab with a stand-in of upsc: try a real UPS, and the
      shutdown for real.

12. Pages for everyday use, what is left
    - The time window of the maintenance tried end to end in the lab: the
      emulated disks are too fast for a sync still running at a set time.
    - The idle action tried on a real machine: the lab only logs it.

13. More arrays, what is left
    - Windows: built from the same sources, but not compiled nor tried.
    - Pools saved in fstab: an explicit order after their branches for
      systemd, on top of "nofail" and of the protected mount points.
    - Per-array metrics in one scrape, with the array as label.


Out of scope
============

- Real-time parity: a different engine from the snapshot parity of SnapRAID.
- Docker, virtual machines, application catalog, remote access service.
- Management of ZFS pools. Detect and show them only.
- Cloud backup: point to restic and rclone.
